      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Checking Kemsit's consonants against candidate Kms confirms a match, since the feminine t-ending can be omitted. Now I'm testing candidate Kꜣ-msw, where the ꜣ can function as a vowel or drop out, and the w can also be omitted, leaving the core consonants k-m-s aligned with the name.\n\nFor Kms=j-w, the structure would read as Kemesi-u with the j rendered as the i sound before the t, but the record includes a t that isn't present in this candidate, so this fails since a record can't contain a consonant the candidate lacks.\n\nFor Kwms, the w produces \"Kumes\" which is a distinct name from Kemsit rather than a variant omission. If Kemsit's t is meant as the feminine ending of Kms (making it Kms.t), then the candidate Kms strictly lacks that t, so by the rules this should fail too—though practically Kms and its feminine form Kmst would be treated as separate names for men versus women.\n\n",
